 {
  term:"Wicking",category:"Fabric performance",
  aliases:["moisture wicking","capillary wicking","wicking rate","vertical wicking","moisture management","liquid moisture transport","one-way transport","directional water transport"],
  definition:"Wicking is the spontaneous transport of liquid through or along a textile by capillary action in the spaces between fibers and yarns.",
  explanation:"Liquid is drawn into narrow gaps when it wets the fiber surfaces, because surface tension pulls the liquid front forward against viscous resistance. In a fabric, those capillaries are the channels between fibers inside yarns and between yarns. Wicking spreads sweat over a larger area where it can evaporate, which is the basis of moisture-management claims. Fiber surface chemistry, cross-section, yarn structure and fabric construction all influence it, and absorbent fibers behave differently from hydrophobic ones.",
  example:"Hang strips of cotton jersey and polyester jersey with their ends in colored water and mark the liquid height every minute. The polyester strip may rise faster, yet when a single drop is placed on each fabric the outcome can differ. One study found that a hydrophobic profiled polyester led in the vertical test, while a hydrophilic-hydrophobic combination yarn spread liquid better under limited droplet supply.",
  caution:"Wicking is not absorption, drying rate or comfort by itself. A fabric can wick quickly yet hold water, or absorb much yet spread slowly. Results depend strongly on test conditions such as liquid supply, direction and time, so compare values only from the same method. Hydrophilic finishes that create wicking on synthetics can wash out.",
  deeper:"Capillary pressure scales with liquid surface tension and the cosine of the contact angle divided by pore radius, so good wetting and fine, well-connected pores drive flow; viscous drag grows as pores narrow. Classical Lucas-Washburn analysis predicts that early wicking distance increases roughly with the square root of time, slowing as gravity or supply limits intervene. Tests include vertical strip wicking with an unlimited reservoir, horizontal or droplet spreading with limited liquid, and moisture management testers measuring one-way transport between faces. Research shows these conditions can rank the same fabrics differently. Engineered structures, such as Janus fabrics with wettability gradients or conical micropores, drive directional transport from skin side to outer face, and reviews now link wicking with evaporation and heat transfer in personal thermal management.",
  origins:"The word borrows the image of a lamp or candle wick drawing fuel upward by capillarity. As synthetic fibers entered sportswear, manufacturers used profiled cross-sections and hydrophilic finishes to promote wicking, and the term moved from physics into marketing. Current research has moved beyond simple wicking and drying toward directional sweat transport and responsive moisture regulation.",
  related:["Moisture regain","Microfiber","Jersey","Finishing","Hand"],
  refs:[
   {title:"Polymers: Liquid transport on twisted profile filament/spun combination yarn in knitted fabric (Cui et al., 2025)",url:"https://doi.org/10.3390/polym17152065"},
   {title:"Advanced Materials: Personal moisture management by advanced textiles and intelligent wearables (Shou and Li, 2025)",url:"https://doi.org/10.1002/adma.202508831"},
   {title:"Advanced Materials: Bioinspired Janus textile with conical micropores for moisture and thermal management (Dai et al., 2019)",url:"https://doi.org/10.1002/adma.201904113"},
   {title:"Heliyon: Fiber types and fabric structures influence on weft knitted fabrics (Hoque et al., 2022)",url:"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2022.e09605"}
  ],
  evidence:"verified"
 },
 {
  term:"Wool Products Labeling Act",category:"Labeling & law",
  aliases:["WPLA","Wool Act","Wool Products Labeling Act of 1939","15 U.S.C. 68","16 CFR Part 300","recycled wool labeling"],
  definition:"A 1939 US federal statute, 15 U.S.C. 68 et seq., requiring wool products to disclose wool, recycled wool and other fiber content by percentage of weight.",
  explanation:"The Act requires wool products to be labeled with the percentage by weight of wool, recycled wool and each other fiber present, together with the name or registered identification number of a responsible company and the country of origin. The Federal Trade Commission enforces it through rules at 16 CFR Part 300. Its defined categories carry the weight: 'recycled wool' means fiber returned to a fibrous state after wool was woven or felted into a product that no consumer ever used, or after wool was spun, woven, knitted or felted into a product that a consumer did use.",
  example:"A coating fabric is made partly from new fleece and partly from cutting-room offcuts of a woven wool fabric that were torn back into fiber. Under the Act's definitions, the offcut-derived portion counts as recycled wool even though no consumer ever wore the fabric. A useful exercise is to sort sample inputs, such as scoured fleece, woven offcuts and shredded worn garments, into the statutory categories before reading any label.",
  caution:"The statutory 'recycled wool' category does not line up neatly with the pre-consumer and post-consumer vocabulary of voluntary standards, because it groups certain unused manufacturing waste with used material. A wool label also says nothing about fiber diameter, breed, animal-welfare practices such as mulesing, or shrink-resist treatment. Specialty fibers such as cashmere have their own conditions in the law, so read the definitions before comparing products.",
  deeper:"The Act's definition of wool reaches beyond sheep and lamb fleece to include hair of the Angora and Cashmere goats and certain specialty fibers from camelids, which may be named by animal on labels. Labels list constituent fibers in order of predominance by weight, and descriptors implying newness, such as 'virgin wool', are restricted to wool that has never been reclaimed. Enforcement depends partly on fiber analysis: scale pattern and diameter under the microscope can separate some animal fibers, but distinguishing recycled from new wool in a finished fabric is difficult by laboratory means alone, so purchase and production records carry much of the evidentiary weight. The Act has been amended by Public Law 96-242 and Public Law 109-428, and the FTC has revised Part 300 through later rulemaking.",
  origins:"Congress enacted the Act in 1939, nearly two decades before the general textile statute of 1958. Its categories reflect a long-standing concern in the wool trade with separating reclaimed wool, historically sold as shoddy, from new fleece so that buyers could tell the two apart. The FTC has periodically reviewed the Part 300 rules, issuing final rule revisions that also touched the fur and textile rules.",
  related:["Textile Fiber Products Identification Act","Generic fiber name","Pre-consumer","Post-consumer","Super S numbers"],
  refs:[
   {title:"eCFR: 16 CFR Part 300, Rules and Regulations Under the Wool Products Labeling Act of 1939",url:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-16/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-300"},
   {title:"Federal Trade Commission: Wool Products Labeling Act (text)",url:"https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/rules/wool-products-labeling-act-text"},
   {title:"Federal Trade Commission: 16 CFR Parts 300, 301 and 303 final rules",url:"https://search.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/federal-register-notices/16-cfr-parts-300-301-303-rules-regulations-under-wool-products-labeling-act-1939-rules-regulations"}
  ],
  evidence:"verified"
 },
 {
  term:"Yarn count",category:"Measurements",
  aliases:["count","yarn number","yarn size","cotton count","Ne","NeC","English count","metric count","Nm","worsted count","direct count","indirect count"],
  definition:"Yarn count is a number expressing a yarn's fineness as length per unit mass (indirect systems such as Ne and Nm) or mass per unit length (direct systems such as tex and denier).",
  explanation:"Two families of count coexist. Indirect systems count how many standard lengths weigh a fixed mass, so a higher number means a finer yarn: English cotton count Ne counts 840-yard hanks per pound, metric count Nm counts kilometers per kilogram, and worsted count counts 560-yard hanks per pound. Direct systems, tex and denier, give mass per fixed length, so a higher number means a coarser yarn. Mills, spinners and retailers still quote whichever system their sector grew up with, which is why the system must always be named alongside the number.",
  example:"A shirting yarn is described as 30s cotton. In Ne that means thirty 840-yard hanks weigh one pound. Converting gives about 19.7 tex, about 177 denier and about Nm 51. A student comparing it with a knitwear yarn sold as Nm 2/48 should first recognize that the second number is metric and describes two plies of Nm 48 singles, resulting in roughly Nm 24 before twist contraction.",
  caution:"A bare number such as 40 is meaningless without its system: 40 Ne is a fine cotton yarn, while 40 tex is a much heavier one. Ply notation also differs between trades and regions, so confirm whether a quoted figure is the singles count or the resultant plied count. Count alone does not describe fiber quality, twist, strength or evenness.",
  deeper:"Definitions give the conversions. Ne equals length in yards divided by 840 times mass in pounds; Nm equals length in meters divided by mass in grams. Because tex is grams per 1,000 meters, Nm equals 1,000 divided by tex, and Ne equals about 590.5 divided by tex; Ne is also Nm divided by about 1.693. For worsted count, the 560-yard hank gives tex of roughly 886 divided by the worsted count. Plied yarns in indirect systems combine reciprocally: two singles of count N give a nominal resultant near N divided by 2, whereas in tex the singles values add. ASTM D2260 tabulates conversion factors and equivalent yarn numbers across numbering systems, consistent with ISO 2947.",
  origins:"Indirect counts grew out of reeling practice: yarn was wound into hanks of a customary length, and a spinner's count recorded how many hanks made up a pound. Different sectors fixed different hank lengths, which is why cotton uses 840 yards and worsted 560 yards. Standardization bodies later framed tex as a common metric replacement and published conversion tables so that traditional counts could be translated rather than abandoned overnight.",
  related:["Tex","Denier","Spinning","Twist","Worsted"],
  refs:[
   {title:"ASTM: D2260 Standard Tables of Conversion Factors and Equivalent Yarn Numbers",url:"https://www.astm.org/Standards/D2260.htm"},
   {title:"Textile School: Yarn count conversions, formulas and conversion factors",url:"https://www.textileschool.com/179/yarn-count-conversions/"},
   {title:"A&E Gutermann: Textile unit calculator and numbering systems",url:"https://industry.guetermann.com/en/service/textile-unit-calculator/"},
   {title:"ISO: ISO 1144:2016 Universal system for designating linear density (Tex System)",url:"https://www.iso.org/standard/70774.html"}
  ],
  evidence:"verified"
 }
